WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:04.040
Phillip: Different organisations will
have different policies and procedures,

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.240
but there are some standard principles
that tend to apply everywhere.

00:00:09.059 --> 00:00:13.169
As a Team Leader you should be fully aware
of these to be effective in your role.

00:00:14.179 --> 00:00:16.329
Maddy: Disciplinary and Dismissal procedures.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:21.750
When it comes to disciplinary and dismissal
policies, your first responsibility as

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:26.520
a team leader is to take the time to
understand your own organisation’s policies.

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:31.300
Talking to HR or your own manager
is a great place to start.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:36.129
It’s really important you understand
this because, as a team leader, you’re

00:00:36.129 --> 00:00:40.480
likely to be the first person involved
in any disciplinary issues in your team.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:51.360
ACAS provides standardized policy and process
templates, that lots of organisations use

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.000
or refer to, in forming their policies, so
you might want to take a look at them too.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.250
Phillip: Disciplinary procedures are in place
to make sure that everyone respects each other

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:06.379
and their employer’s rules and values, and
that everyone’s work is to the right standard.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.009
Disciplinary procedures aim to solve problems
and prevent them from happening again and

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:14.000
shouldn’t be thought of as ways to punish people.

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:17.170
Maddy: Dismissal procedures
are there as a last resort.

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:21.659
Generally, organisations don’t want
to have to dismiss their people unless

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.969
they have to, because it can cause a lot
of disruption and emotional distress.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:31.540
But there are situations where
dismissal is necessary and unavoidable.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:33.900
Phillip: Taking the Informal approach

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:41.020
When a disciplinary issue occurs, if possible, the
best way to address it initially is informally.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:43.940
This saves everyone time and energy.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:49.460
So, if things aren’t going right for someone
in your team, don’t rush to trigger the

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:51.940
formal disciplinary process right away.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:57.999
You need to be cautious, patient, and
understanding of everyone involved.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.469
Maddy: Start by arranging to have a private
conversation with the person or people involved.

00:02:04.209 --> 00:02:08.830
You shouldn’t go into this with any biases about
who you think is in the right or in the wrong.

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:12.910
That would affect your judgement and
won’t help resolve the situation.

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:16.359
Phillip: Make sure you listen to
everyone’s point of view neutrally.

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:21.380
People will have different opinions
about even simple disciplinary matters,

00:02:21.589 --> 00:02:25.920
so it’s really important that you
listen to everyone and stay impartial.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:31.750
Maddy: Aim to create an agreement about what
changes or improvements need to be made.

00:02:32.599 --> 00:02:37.149
If the issue is to do with someone having an
inappropriate attitude at work, you might take

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:42.090
time to find out where this is coming from and
what changes can be made to make this better.

00:02:42.889 --> 00:02:46.490
If the disciplinary issue is to do
with performance, you might create a

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:48.260
development plan for the person involved.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:54.829
The important thing is to focus on finding
ways to improve the situation, not to

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:56.930
pass down judgements and punishments.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:02:59.769
Phillip: Time for the Formal Process

00:03:01.059 --> 00:03:05.600
When we say that it’s generally better to try
and deal with disciplinary issues informally

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:10.370
first, this doesn’t mean you should try to
sweep difficult issues under the carpet.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:15.959
Maddy: There are some issues, like bullying,
harassment, discrimination, and other acts of

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:21.030
gross misconduct, where the formal disciplinary
process is the correct one to follow.

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:25.920
This wouldn’t mean you’d abandon all of
the elements of informally tackling the

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:30.600
issue that we’ve described; it just means
you’ve made an appropriate escalation

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.140
according to your organisation’s policies.

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:38.060
Phillip: You can see that as a leader it’s very
important that you’re an expert in your own

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:43.660
organisation’s procedures and policies because
members of your team may come to you for advice.

00:03:44.139 --> 00:03:48.540
So, remember If you’re ever unsure
of anything, make sure that you

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.520
find it out before someone asks you.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:55.520
That will help you to be in a position
to help your team, as well as knowing how

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.489
to navigate these situations yourself.

